>> title: Sha Shen and Yu Zhu Lean Pork Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, solomon's seal, adenophora, honey dates, cantonese, old-fire soup, autumn, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 沙參玉竹瘦肉湯, saa sam juk zuk sau juk tong, sa sum yuk juk sau yuk tong, sha shen and yu zhu lean pork soup, adenophora and Solomon's seal soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 4
>> time: 3 hr
>> slack: forgiving — salting it at the start draws the pork dry and leaves the broth thin, and no salt later puts that back
>> step.1: Autumn dryness — 潤燥 — and the standard demonstration that a 老火湯 does not have to be big. Four things. 沙參 and 玉竹 are sold and used as one pair, and a pot with only one of them is a different soup.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the pork
>> step.3: soak the pair 20 min
>> step.4: simmer 2 1/2 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

Autumn dryness — 潤燥 — and the standard demonstration that a 老火湯 does not have to be big. Four things. 沙參 and 玉竹 are sold and used as one pair, and a pot with only one of them is a different soup.

Blanch @lean pork{1%lb}(450 g; sau juk, in two whole pieces — the light body for a pot meant to taste of the dried goods) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{8%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse the pork under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Soak @adenophora root{1%oz}(30 g; saa sam, the pale dried root) and @Solomon's seal{1%oz}(30 g; juk zuk, rhizome slices — for a dry throat and a dry cough, and the autumn word is 潤燥) with @honey dates{2}(mat zou; the only sweetness in the pot) in cold water, ~soak{20%min}, then lift them out and leave the grit behind.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched pork{} with @&(~1)soaked roots{} and @cold water{2 1/2%qt}(2.4 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{2 1/2%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{3/4%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
